      "title": "Translation — mRNA to Protein",
      "competency": "STEM_BIO11/12-IIIa-b-5",
      "content": "<h3>🎯 Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Describe the process of translation.</li><li>Explain the roles of mRNA, tRNA, and rRNA.</li><li>Use the codon table.</li><li>Diagram protein synthesis.</li></ul><h3>🔑 Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>Translation</strong> — mRNA → protein.</li><li><strong>tRNA</strong> — brings amino acids to ribosome.</li><li><strong>Anticodon</strong> — pairs with mRNA codon.</li><li><strong>Ribosome</strong> — site of translation.</li></ul><h3>📚 Lesson Content</h3><h4>A. Steps of Translation</h4><ol><li><strong>Initiation:</strong> Ribosome binds mRNA at the start codon (AUG)</li><li><strong>Elongation:</strong> tRNA brings amino acids; peptide bonds form</li><li><strong>Termination:</strong> Stop codon reached; polypeptide released</li></ol><h4>B. Codon Table</h4><ul><li>AUG = Methionine (start)</li><li>UAA, UAG, UGA = Stop</li><li>20 amino acids encoded by 64 codons</li></ul><h4>C. Example</h4><p>mRNA: AUG CCU AAG → Met–Pro–Lys</p>",
